---
/** The random deck: a live generator on top, and under it a short
 * walkthrough of the query API using the very file the page runs.
 * The form's own code lives in deck-page.js and stays out of the
 * tutorial. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import source from "../../fun/random-deck.js?raw";
import { loadRegistry, orderedSets } from "../../data/registry";

/** The tutorial file, split at its "// ── N." headings. */
const sections = source.split(/^(?=\/\/ ── )/m).map((chunk) => chunk.trim());
const step = (n: number) => sections.find((s) => s.startsWith(`// ── ${n}.`)) ?? "";
const usage = `const choice = {
  elements: ["Fire", "Water"],
  sets: ["001", "006"],
  toolbox: 3,
  split: { Artifact: 8, Aura: 6, Magic: 16, Minion: 27 },
};

const deck = await makeDeck(choice);

console.log(deck);`;
const repo = "https://github.com/sadkinglabs/kairos-archive/blob/main/src/fun";
const elements = ["Air", "Earth", "Fire", "Water"];
const split = { Artifact: 8, Aura: 6, Magic: 16, Minion: 30 };
const { registry } = await loadRegistry();
const sets = orderedSets(registry.sets).filter((s) => s.set_code && s.set_code !== "999");
---
<Base title="Random deck" canonical="/fun/random-deck" description="A random Sorcery deck from the Kairos Archive query API, and a short tutorial: build a query, fetch the pages, use the card records with ordinary JavaScript.">
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/fun">Fun Zone</a></p>
  <h1>Random deck</h1>
  <p class="intro">Pick elements and sets, say how the spellbook splits and whether Toolbox is in, and deal. Every part of the deck is one search query, and the list shows the query each part came from and how many cards it matched. Under the generator, the code that does it, section by section.</p>

  <form id="deck-form" class="deck-form">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Elements</legend>
      <div class="row">{elements.map((e) => <label><input type="checkbox" name="element" value={e} checked={e === "Fire" || e === "Water"} /> {e}</label>)}</div>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Sets</legend>
      <div class="row">{sets.map((s) => <label><input type="checkbox" name="set" value={s.set_code} checked /> {s.set_name}</label>)}</div>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Spellbook</legend>
      <div class="row">{Object.entries(split).map(([type, n]) => <label>{type}s <input type="number" name={type} value={n} min="0" max="60" /></label>)}</div>
    </fieldset>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Toolbox</legend>
      <div class="row">{[0, 1, 2, 3].map((n) => <label><input type="radio" name="toolbox" value={n} checked={n === 0} /> {n}</label>)}</div>
      <output id="deck-total" class="total" for="Artifact Aura Magic Minion toolbox" aria-live="polite">60 / 60 spells</output>
    </fieldset>
    <button type="submit">Deal a deck</button>
  </form>
  <pre id="deck" class="rules deck" aria-live="polite">Your deck appears here.</pre>

  <section class="tutorial">
  <h2>Build a random Sorcery deck with the Kairos API</h2>
  <p>Kairos Archive exposes the same search system used by the website through a simple JSON API. You do not need an API key or a JavaScript library. In this example we search for cards, handle pagination, and use the returned records to generate a random deck. The code below is the file this page runs, in plain JavaScript.</p>

  <h3>1. Describe the deck</h3>
  <p>A few constants for the rules the deck must follow, and the shape of a choice: which elements, which sets, how many Toolbox, and how the sixty spells split by type. This is your data, not the API's.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(1)} lang="javascript" title="1 · the rules and a choice" />

  <h3>2. Turn those choices into a Kairos query</h3>
  <p>One query per part of the deck, in the same syntax you can type into the search box at the top of this page. <code>t:minion (e:fire,water or e:none) s:001,006 is:booster</code> reads: minions, with Fire or Water or no element, printed in a booster of Alpha or Gothic. The full <a href="/syntax">syntax</a> is one page.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(2)} lang="javascript" title="2 · makeQuery" />

  <h3>3. Fetch matching cards</h3>
  <p>This is the whole API. <code>fetch()</code> calls <code>/cards?q=…</code>, <code>body.data</code> is an array of card records, and <code>body.has_more</code> says whether another page exists. Pages hold up to two hundred cards, so the loop asks until there are no more.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(3)} lang="javascript" title="3 · getCards" />

  <h3>4. Use the card records</h3>
  <p>From here on it is ordinary JavaScript over ordinary objects. Each record has <code>name</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>rarity</code>, <code>elements</code> and the rest of the <a href="/docs/types/card">card object</a>. Two things this deck needs: a query like <code>e:fire,water</code> means Fire <em>or</em> Water, so a Fire and Air card slips in, and the generator should reject any card with an element the user did not pick. And a draw that respects the copy limits: four of an Ordinary, three of an Exceptional, two of an Elite, one of a Unique.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(4)} lang="javascript" title="4 · onlyChosenElements and drawCards" />

  <h3>5. Assemble the deck</h3>
  <p>The parts in order: the avatar, the spellbook by type, Toolbox, the sites, and, when Toolbox is in, a collection of ten Ordinary spells for it to cast. For each part: build the query, fetch the cards, filter them, draw, add the part to the deck. A card's copies count across the whole deck, so a name already used is skipped.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(5)} lang="javascript" title="5 · makeDeck" />

  <h3>Try it</h3>
  <p>Describe a deck and ask for it. What comes back is an array of parts, each with its query, how many cards matched, and the cards drawn.</p>
  <CodeExample code={usage} lang="javascript" title="usage" />

  <p class="takeaway">Kairos handles finding and structuring the card data. Your application logic can stay ordinary, boring JavaScript.</p>

  <details class="all">
    <summary>See the complete code</summary>
    <p>The file in one block: <a href={`${repo}/random-deck.js`}>random-deck.js</a>. The form above and the printed list are their own file, <a href={`${repo}/deck-page.js`}>deck-page.js</a>, and not part of the tutorial.</p>
    <CodeExample code={`${source}\n\n${usage}`} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js" />
  </details>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  import "../../fun/deck-page.js";
</script>

<style>
  .deck-form { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--card); border-radius: 8px; padding: .75rem 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: .75rem 1.5rem; align-items: start; }
  .deck-form fieldset { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .deck-form legend { font-weight: 600; font-size: .85rem; padding: 0; margin-bottom: .2rem; }
  .deck-form .row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .25rem .9rem; align-items: center; }
  .deck-form .row label { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .3rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .deck-form input[type="number"] { width: 3.6rem; min-height: 34px; padding: .2rem .4rem; }
  .deck-form button { justify-self: start; min-height: 38px; }
  .deck-form button:disabled { opacity: .45; cursor: not-allowed; }
  .deck-form .total { display: block; margin-top: .4rem; font-weight: 600; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .deck-form .total.over { color: var(--removed-fg); }
  .deck-form .total.short { color: var(--warn); }
  .deck { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .takeaway { font-family: var(--display); font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.4; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding-left: 1rem; margin: 2rem 0; }
  /* Room around each code box, and a step's heading well clear of the box before it. */
  .tutorial :global(.code-example) { margin: 1rem 0 2.75rem; }
  .tutorial h3 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .all { margin-top: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .all summary { cursor: pointer; font: 600 1.05rem/1.4 var(--display); color: var(--accent); }
  .all[open] summary { margin-bottom: .75rem; }
</style>
